    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Educated at Harrow and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Nehru gave up the law for the nationalist movement, spent over nine years in British prisons and became, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s backing, the leader who called for complete independence in 1929.</li><li>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first prime minister for nearly seventeen years he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a>, secularism, science and planned industrialisation, and led the Non-Aligned Movement between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> blocs.</li><li>His reputation suffered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s defeat in the 1962 war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and critics fault his economic protectionism, his handling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and what they call his selective secularism.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Nehru's best-known books, among them The Discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Glimpses of World History and An Autobiography, were all written in jail; Letters from a Father to His Daughter was written to Indira, then ten years old.</p></aside>
    </section>

